Feature: SQL Transformation Logic Update for pat_account Table

  The transformation logic for the columns prescriber_id, patient_sf_id, hash_key, and last_modified_date in the pat_account table must be updated to align with the latest source-to-target mapping specifications. This ensures data quality, consistency, and reporting accuracy.

  Background:
    Given the source table purgo_playground.p_account contains valid records
    And the mapping rules are defined in source_target_mapping_specifications.xlsx
    And the target table purgo_playground.pat_account has the following columns:
      | patient_foundation_shipment | prescriber_id | prescriber_key | patient_sf_id | service_request_type | case_sf_id | account_id | hash_key | last_modified_date |
    And the ETL process is executed in Unity Catalog: purgo_databricks, Schema: purgo_playground

  # Happy Path Scenarios

  Scenario: Transform prescriber_id with uppercase and conditional prefix
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = "john smith"
    When the transformation logic is applied to prescriber_id
    Then prescriber_id should be "DR.JOHN SMITH"

  Scenario: Transform prescriber_id when prefix already present (case-insensitive)
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = "Dr.Emily Jones"
    When the transformation logic is applied to prescriber_id
    Then prescriber_id should be "DR.EMILY JONES"

  Scenario: Transform prescriber_id when prefix is present in lowercase
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = "dr.michael brown"
    When the transformation logic is applied to prescriber_id
    Then prescriber_id should be "DR.MICHAEL BROWN"

  Scenario Outline: Transform prescriber_id for various input cases
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = <input>
    When the transformation logic is applied to prescriber_id
    Then prescriber_id should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | input              | expected           |
      | "john smith"       | "DR.JOHN SMITH"    |
      | "Dr.Emily Jones"   | "DR.EMILY JONES"   |
      | "dr.michael brown" | "DR.MICHAEL BROWN" |
      | "DR.ANNA LEE"      | "DR.ANNA LEE"      |
      | "Dr.JOHN DOE"      | "DR.JOHN DOE"      |

  Scenario: Transform patient_sf_id only if patinet_c contains 'PAT'
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "PAT12345"
    When the transformation logic is applied to patient_sf_id
    Then patient_sf_id should be "PAT12345"

  Scenario: Set patient_sf_id to NULL if patinet_c does not contain 'PAT'
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "12345"
    When the transformation logic is applied to patient_sf_id
    Then patient_sf_id should be NULL

  Scenario Outline: Transform patient_sf_id for various patinet_c values
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <input>
    When the transformation logic is applied to patient_sf_id
    Then patient_sf_id should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | input      | expected    |
      | "PAT001"   | "PAT001"    |
      | "PAT999"   | "PAT999"    |
      | "123456"   | NULL        |
      | "XYZPAT"   | "XYZPAT"    |
      | "XYZ123"   | NULL        |

  Scenario: Transform hash_key by concatenating patinet_c, recordtypeid, id with underscore
    Given a source record with patinet_c = "PAT001", recordtypeid = "RT01", id = "C123"
    When the transformation logic is applied to hash_key
    Then hash_key should be "PAT001_RT01_C123"

  Scenario Outline: Transform hash_key for various input values
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <patinet_c>, recordtypeid = <recordtypeid>, id = <id>
    When the transformation logic is applied to hash_key
    Then hash_key should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | patinet_c | recordtypeid | id    | expected           |
      | "PAT001"  | "RT01"       | "C123"| "PAT001_RT01_C123" |
      | "PAT002"  | "RT02"       | "C456"| "PAT002_RT02_C456" |
      | "123456"  | "RT03"       | "C789"| "123456_RT03_C789" |

  Scenario: Set last_modified_date to current ETL execution timestamp
    Given the ETL process is running at "2024-06-10T12:34:56Z"
    When the transformation logic is applied to last_modified_date
    Then last_modified_date should be "2024-06-10T12:34:56Z"

  # Error Scenarios

  Scenario: Error when prescriber_name_c is NULL
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = NULL
    When the transformation logic is applied to prescriber_id
    Then an error should be logged in pat_account_error_log with
      | error_col      | error_message                       |
      | prescriber_id  | "prescriber_name_c is NULL"         |

  Scenario: Error when patinet_c is NULL
    Given a source record with patinet_c = NULL
    When the transformation logic is applied to patient_sf_id
    Then an error should be logged in pat_account_error_log with
      | error_col      | error_message                       |
      | patient_sf_id  | "patinet_c is NULL"                 |

  Scenario: Error when recordtypeid or id is NULL for hash_key
    Given a source record with recordtypeid = NULL or id = NULL
    When the transformation logic is applied to hash_key
    Then an error should be logged in pat_account_error_log with
      | error_col | error_message                        |
      | hash_key  | "recordtypeid or id is NULL"         |

  Scenario: Error when ETL timestamp is not available for last_modified_date
    Given the ETL process does not provide a timestamp
    When the transformation logic is applied to last_modified_date
    Then an error should be logged in pat_account_error_log with
      | error_col         | error_message                        |
      | last_modified_date| "ETL execution timestamp missing"    |

  # Validation Rules

  Scenario Outline: Validate prescriber_id transformation rules
    Given a source record with prescriber_name_c = <input>
    When the transformation logic is applied
    Then prescriber_id must:
      | - be uppercase
      | - start with "DR."
      | - not contain duplicate "DR." prefix
    And prescriber_id should be <expected>
    Examples:
      | input              | expected           |
      | "john smith"       | "DR.JOHN SMITH"    |
      | "Dr.John Smith"    | "DR.JOHN SMITH"    |
      | "DR.JOHN SMITH"    | "DR.JOHN SMITH"    |
      | "dr.john smith"    | "DR.JOHN SMITH"    |

  Scenario Outline: Validate patient_sf_id conditional logic
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <input>
    When the transformation logic is applied
    Then patient_sf_id must be <expected>
    Examples:
      | input      | expected    |
      | "PAT001"   | "PAT001"    |
      | "123456"   | NULL        |

  Scenario Outline: Validate hash_key concatenation and format
    Given a source record with patinet_c = <patinet_c>, recordtypeid = <recordtypeid>, id = <id>
    When the transformation logic is applied
    Then hash_key must be <expected>
    And hash_key must use underscore (_) as delimiter
    And hash_key must not contain NULL values
    Examples:
      | patinet_c | recordtypeid | id    | expected           |
      | "PAT001"  | "RT01"       | "C123"| "PAT001_RT01_C123" |
      | "PAT002"  | "RT02"       | "C456"| "PAT002_RT02_C456" |

  Scenario: Validate last_modified_date is set to ETL execution timestamp
    Given the ETL process is running at "2024-06-10T12:34:56Z"
    When the transformation logic is applied
    Then last_modified_date must be "2024-06-10T12:34:56Z"
    And last_modified_date must be in ISO 8601 format

  # Data-driven Error Logging

  Scenario Outline: Error logging for invalid input data
    Given a source record with <error_col> = <error_value>
    When the transformation logic is applied
    Then an error should be logged in pat_account_error_log with
      | error_col      | error_message                       |
      | <error_col>    | <error_message>                     |
    Examples:
      | error_col         | error_value | error_message                        |
      | prescriber_id     | NULL        | "prescriber_name_c is NULL"          |
      | patient_sf_id     | NULL        | "patinet_c is NULL"                  |
      | hash_key          | NULL        | "recordtypeid or id is NULL"         |
      | last_modified_date| NULL        | "ETL execution timestamp missing"    |

  # Acceptance Criteria

  Scenario: All transformation rules are applied as per mapping specification
    Given the source data and mapping specification
    When the ETL process runs
    Then prescriber_id, patient_sf_id, hash_key, and last_modified_date in pat_account must conform to the rules:
      | prescriber_id      | Uppercase, prefixed with "DR." only if not present, no duplicate prefix |
      | patient_sf_id      | Value only if patinet_c contains "PAT", else NULL                      |
      | hash_key           | Concatenation of patinet_c, recordtypeid, id with underscore (_)       |
      | last_modified_date | Set to ETL execution timestamp in ISO 8601 format                      |
    And any error in transformation must be logged in pat_account_error_log with appropriate error message

